import AnalysisLayout from '../../layouts/AnalysisLayout.astro';
---

<AnalysisLayout
  title="SBB / integrated communications audit"
  description="Audit of a telecoms operator's integrated marketing communications, with stakeholder mapping, a public attitudes survey, measurable KPIs and a crisis response plan."
  heading="sbb."
  course="Integrated Business Communications, MSc"
  year="2022"
  scope="team project, four people"
  tools={['Stakeholder mapping', 'Competitor benchmarking', 'Survey design', 'SWOT', 'KPI definition', 'Risk and crisis planning']}
>

  <h2>the brief</h2>

  <p>
    Audit how a large company actually communicates, across every channel and every audience, then build a strategic
    communications plan on top of what the audit finds. The company was SBB.
  </p>

  <h2>approach</h2>

  <h3>Establish the position with real market data</h3>
  <p>
    Market share came from the national regulator rather than from the company's own materials, taken from Q4 2021:
  </p>

  <div class="rc-stats">
    <div class="rc-stat"><span class="rc-stat-n">44.8%</span><span class="rc-stat-l">media distribution</span></div>
    <div class="rc-stat"><span class="rc-stat-n">31.4%</span><span class="rc-stat-l">internet</span></div>
    <div class="rc-stat"><span class="rc-stat-n">22.0%</span><span class="rc-stat-l">fixed telephony</span></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Against a single dominant competitor across all three categories. That framed the whole plan, because it meant
    the objective was recovering a leadership position rather than defending one.
  </p>

  <p>
    Company figures came from the business registry rather than the press pack: 1,728 employees at the close of the
    year, an internal survey showing 80% of staff with a strong sense of belonging and 38% of managers under 40.
  </p>

  <h3>Map every audience separately</h3>
  <p>
    Six distinct publics: employees, private customers, business customers, educational institutions, state
    institutions and media. Each was given its own channels, its own message and its own relationship approach,
    rather than one message pushed everywhere.
  </p>

  <h3>Specify what to hold on each of them</h3>
  <p>
    The plan did not stop at naming the publics. It specified what the PR database should store for each one and
    what every field is there to trigger: employment dates and children's birthdays for staff, so work anniversaries
    and school starts can be marked; contract and founding dates for business customers, so renewals and milestones
    are not missed; viewing preferences and birthdays for private customers; student performance data for the
    universities. No field goes in unless something happens as a result of it.
  </p>

  <h3>Test what the public actually believes</h3>
  <p>
    We ran our own survey, 21 respondents via Google Forms, rating nine dimensions of the company on a 1 to 5 scale
    and answering open questions about what should improve.
  </p>

  <div class="rc-result">
    <p class="rc-result-label">the finding that changed the plan</p>
    <p>
      <strong>81%</strong> of respondents said they knew what the company logo looked like. Only <strong>38.1%</strong>
      could correctly identify its colours. Stated brand recognition was roughly double actual brand recognition,
      which turned "raise awareness of the visual identity" from a generic objective into a measured gap with a
      number attached.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The ratings were mostly positive, with one consistent exception: internet signal quality drew real
    dissatisfaction and it showed up again unprompted in the open comments. So it went into the plan as an
    operational issue, not a messaging one. You cannot communicate your way out of a connection that drops.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two other numbers from the same survey were worth having. <strong>55%</strong> of respondents used none of the
    bundled extra services at all, with 35% on HBO MAX, 10% on Tidal and nobody at all using three of the others.
    Yet <strong>40%</strong> were on the most expensive package, more than on either cheaper one. People were paying
    for the premium bundle and not opening most of what came with it, which is a retention problem dressed as a
    pricing success.
  </p>

  <h3>Commit to numbers</h3>
  <p>
    The plan set measurable targets rather than intentions: 50+ media placements, four million people reached by a
    promotional video, +17% market share, +34% follower growth, 81% visual identity recognition, +25% satisfaction
    among internet users, +13% intranet newsletter views and five new stakeholder relationships within six months.
  </p>

  <h3>Plan for it going wrong</h3>
  <p>
    Three "what if" scenarios with prepared responses: the main competitor gaining share on price, the anniversary
    promotional video being read the wrong way and false information about the company circulating in the media.
    Each with a specific response rather than a general principle.
  </p>

  <h2>what came out of it</h2>

  <p>
    A full communications plan covering internal communications, media relations, direct marketing, corporate
    identity and special events, all tied back to the seven stated objectives and the KPI set. The brand
    recommendation was a logo refresh timed to the company's twentieth anniversary, carrying a new line.
  </p>

  <p>
    Media relations were tiered rather than kept as one flat list: partner titles inside the group, gift-based
    relationships with the large general and technology portals, then straightforward professional contact with the
    specialist journals. Internal communications and special events were given the same treatment, down to named
    dates and a named public figure fronting the environmental campaign.
  </p>

  <p class="rc-note">
    A team of four. The work was shared across the whole thing rather than split into separate sections.
  </p>

</AnalysisLayout>
